# Design an Interactive Research Presentation

### Section Preview

Before you begin, here is what you'll be building — a clickable multi-screen research presentation where viewers navigate between sections, zoom into figures, and explore your findings interactively:

![Final Interactive Presentation Design](images/academic-presentation-final.png)

---

## Why Figma for academic presentations?

PowerPoint gives you slides. Figma gives you an **experience**. With Figma's prototype features you can build a presentation where:

* Clicking a research question jumps directly to that finding
* Clicking a figure zooms it in full screen
* Hovering over a term shows a tooltip definition
* A navigation bar lets the audience jump to any section at any time

This is not a gimmick — it changes how an audience engages with your research. Instead of sitting through 20 linear slides, they can explore. It's especially powerful for complex multi-study papers, thesis defences, and grant presentations where the audience has different levels of familiarity with your work.

---

## Learning goals

By the end of this activity, you will be able to:

* Build a **multi-screen Figma prototype** with at least 5 linked screens.
* Use **Smart Animate** to create smooth screen-to-screen transitions.
* Design an **interactive navigation bar** that appears on every screen.
* Create a **figure zoom interaction** — click a chart to see it full screen.
* Build a **tooltip component** for defining key terms on hover.
* Export a **shareable prototype link** your audience can open in any browser — no PowerPoint, no Keynote, no software required.

---

## 1) Plan your presentation structure

Before opening Figma, sketch your 5 screens on paper. A research presentation needs:

| Screen | Purpose |
|---|---|
| **Title screen** | Title, authors, institution, conference name |
| **Overview screen** | Clickable map of your presentation — each section is a button |
| **Methods screen** | Your study design, participants, measures |
| **Results screen** | Key findings with figures |
| **Conclusion screen** | Takeaways, limitations, next steps, contact |

Write down your own content for each screen before you start building. The activity goes much faster when you're not writing and designing at the same time.

---

## 2) Set up your file and frame size

Presentations display on widescreen monitors. We'll design for **1920 × 1080px** (full HD).

1. Open Figma and create a new **Design File**. Name it `Research Presentation — [Your Name]`.
2. Press **F** to activate the Frame tool.
3. In the **Right Sidebar**, select the **Presentation** preset — or set a custom size:
   * **Width:** 1920
   * **Height:** 1080
4. Name this frame `01 — Title` in the Layers panel.
5. Set the background color to `#0D1B2A` (deep navy — same family as the poster for a consistent academic look).
6. Duplicate this frame 4 times (Ctrl/Cmd + D × 4) and name them:
   * `02 — Overview`
   * `03 — Methods`
   * `04 — Results`
   * `05 — Conclusion`

> **Layout tip:** Arrange all 5 frames side by side horizontally on your canvas with 80px gaps between them. This lets you see the full presentation at a glance and makes prototyping connections much easier.

---

## 3) Build the persistent navigation bar

A navigation bar that appears on every screen is what makes this feel like a real interactive experience rather than just a slide deck.

**Create the nav bar on the Title screen:**

1. Press **R** and draw a rectangle at the bottom of the `01 — Title` frame:
   * **Width:** 1920
   * **Height:** 72
   * **Y position:** 1008 (flush with bottom)
   * **Fill:** `#1A3A5C`
2. Press **T** and add 5 text labels inside the nav bar, evenly spaced:
   * `Title` · `Overview` · `Methods` · `Results` · `Conclusion`
   * **Font:** Inter, Size 24, Weight 500, Color `#A8C4E0`
3. Add a thin top border to the nav bar:
   * Select the rectangle → **Stroke** → Top only → Color `#2E86C1` → Weight 2px
4. Select everything in the nav bar and press **Shift + A** to apply Auto Layout. Set direction to Horizontal, spacing to **Equal** (space between mode).
5. Name this group `Nav Bar` in the Layers panel.

**Copy the nav bar to all other screens:**

1. Select the `Nav Bar` group.
2. Copy it (Ctrl/Cmd + C).
3. Click into `02 — Overview`, paste (Ctrl/Cmd + V), and position it at the same Y position (1008).
4. Repeat for screens 03, 04, and 05.

> **Pro tip:** If you later change the nav bar design, you'll need to update all 5 copies. To avoid this, consider turning the nav bar into a **Component** (Ctrl/Cmd + Alt + K) before copying — any changes to the main component will automatically update all instances.

---

## 4) Design the Title screen

The title screen sets the tone. Keep it bold and minimal.

1. Select the `01 — Title` frame.
2. Draw a vertical accent bar on the left edge:
   * **Width:** 12
   * **Height:** 400
   * **X:** 0, **Y:** 340
   * **Fill:** `#2E86C1`
3. Press **T** and add your presentation title:
   * **Font:** Inter, Size 72, Weight 700, Color `#FFFFFF`
   * **Width:** 1400 (leave right margin)
   * Position at X: 80, Y: 360
   * Example: `Makerspace Participation and Student Employment: A Mixed-Methods Study`
4. Add author names below:
   * Size 36, Weight 400, Color `#A8C4E0`
   * Example: `Jane Smith · David Lee · Rich McCue`
5. Add institution and conference below that:
   * Size 28, Color `#7A9BB5`
   * Example: `University of Victoria · ICET 2025`
6. In the bottom-right corner, add a small text block:
   * `Press any key or click to begin` — Size 20, Color `#7A9BB5`
   * This signals to the audience that the presentation is interactive.

---

## 5) Design the Overview screen

The overview screen is what makes this presentation fundamentally different from PowerPoint. It's a **clickable map** of your entire presentation.

1. Select the `02 — Overview` frame.
2. Add a screen title at the top: `Presentation overview` — Size 56, Bold, Color `#FFFFFF`, Y position 80.
3. Create 4 large clickable section cards (one for each content screen):

   For each card:
   * Press **R** → draw a rectangle: **Width:** 380, **Height:** 240, **Corner radius:** 16
   * Fill: `#1A3A5C`
   * Add a top accent border: Stroke top only, `#2E86C1`, 4px
   * Press **T** → add section number: Size 48, Bold, Color `#2E86C1`
   * Add section name below: Size 32, Weight 500, Color `#FFFFFF`
   * Add a one-line description: Size 20, Color `#A8C4E0`

   Card content:
   * **01 Methods** — `Study design, participants, measures`
   * **02 Results** — `Key findings and figures`
   * **03 Discussion** — `Interpretation and implications`
   * **04 Conclusion** — `Takeaways and next steps`

4. Arrange the 4 cards in a 2×2 grid, centered on screen. Use Auto Layout for the grid: select all 4 cards → Shift + A → set Horizontal spacing 40, then wrap into two rows.
5. Add a subtle instruction below the grid: `Click any section to jump directly to it` — Size 20, Color `#7A9BB5`

---

## 6) Design the Methods screen

1. Select the `03 — Methods` frame.
2. Add a section label in the top-left: `Methods` — Size 56, Bold, Color `#2E86C1`
3. Add a thin horizontal line below it: Color `#2E86C1`, 2px stroke, Width 400
4. Divide the screen into two halves: Left = study design text, Right = participant diagram

   **Left half — study design:**
   * Draw a content area: X 80, Y 160, Width 800, Height 750
   * Add your methods text in organized blocks with section sub-headers in `#2E86C1` and body text in `#D4E6F1`, Size 28, Line height 1.7

   **Right half — participant breakdown:**
   * Draw 3 stacked cards (X 960, each 840px wide, 220px tall, 20px gaps):
   * Card 1: `Survey phase` — `n = 342 undergraduate students`
   * Card 2: `Interview phase` — `n = 24 selected participants`
   * Card 3: `Employment data` — `Co-op records 2021–2024`
   * Style each card: Fill `#1A3A5C`, corner radius 12, left border accent `#2E86C1` 4px

---

## 7) Design the Results screen with a zoomable figure

This is where Figma's prototype power really shows. We'll make a chart that zooms full screen when clicked.

1. Select the `04 — Results` frame.
2. Add the section label `Results` and divider line (same style as Methods).
3. Add your key findings as large-number stat cards across the top (same style as the poster activity — big numbers at Size 96, descriptions at Size 24).
4. Below the stat cards, add a **figure placeholder**:
   * Press **R** → draw a rectangle: Width 900, Height 420, Corner radius 12
   * Fill `#1A3A5C`
   * Add a text label inside: `Figure 1: Co-op placement rates by makerspace engagement level` — Size 20, Color `#7A9BB5`, centered
   * Add a small blue label in the top-right corner of the figure: `Click to enlarge` — Size 16, Color `#2E86C1`

**Create the zoomed figure screen:**

1. Duplicate the `04 — Results` frame and name it `04b — Figure Zoom`.
2. On this screen, delete everything except the nav bar.
3. Make the figure fill most of the screen: Width 1760, Height 900, centered.
4. Add a close button in the top-right: draw a circle (48×48px, Fill `#1A3A5C`), add an X text label inside (Size 24, Color `#FFFFFF`).
5. Add a dark semi-transparent overlay behind the figure: Full screen rectangle, Fill `#0D1B2A` at 85% opacity.

We'll connect these two screens with a prototype interaction in Section 9.

---

## 8) Design the Conclusion screen

1. Select the `05 — Conclusion` frame.
2. Add the section label `Conclusion` and divider.
3. Divide into two columns:

   **Left — key takeaways:**
   * 3 takeaway cards stacked vertically, each with a numbered accent circle and two lines of text
   * Card style: Fill `#1A3A5C`, corner radius 12, padding 24px
   * Numbered circle: 48×48px, Fill `#2E86C1`, number in white Size 24 Bold

   **Right — next steps + contact:**
   * `Next steps` sub-header + 3 bullet points
   * A contact card at the bottom:
     * Your name, email, lab website
     * A QR code image (generate one at qr-code-generator.com → download PNG → insert as image fill in a 160×160px square)
   * `Scan for full paper and data` label below the QR code

---

## 9) Add prototype connections

Now we wire everything together. This is what turns a static design into an interactive presentation.

1. Click the **Prototype** tab in the Right Sidebar (top right area of Figma).
2. You'll see a blue dot appear on the right edge of elements when you hover over them — drag from this dot to another frame to create a connection.

**Wire these connections:**

| From | Trigger | To | Animation |
|---|---|---|---|
| `01 — Title` (click anywhere) | On click | `02 — Overview` | Smart Animate, 400ms, Ease Out |
| `Overview` card 01 Methods | On click | `03 — Methods` | Smart Animate, 400ms, Ease Out |
| `Overview` card 02 Results | On click | `04 — Results` | Smart Animate, 400ms, Ease Out |
| `Overview` card 04 Conclusion | On click | `05 — Conclusion` | Smart Animate, 400ms, Ease Out |
| `Figure 1` rectangle | On click | `04b — Figure Zoom` | Smart Animate, 300ms, Ease In |
| Close button on zoom screen | On click | `04 — Results` | Smart Animate, 300ms, Ease Out |

**Wire the nav bar on every screen:**

On each screen's nav bar, connect each label to its corresponding frame:
* `Title` text → `01 — Title`
* `Overview` text → `02 — Overview`
* `Methods` text → `03 — Methods`
* `Results` text → `04 — Results`
* `Conclusion` text → `05 — Conclusion`

> **Important:** You need to wire the nav bar on each screen separately — Figma prototype connections don't carry over when you copy a frame.

---

## 10) Test and share your prototype

1. Click the **Play (▶) button** in the top-right corner of Figma to launch the prototype preview.
2. Test every connection:
   * Does clicking the title screen advance to Overview?
   * Do all nav bar buttons work on every screen?
   * Does clicking Figure 1 zoom it full screen?
   * Does the close button return to Results?
3. Fix any broken connections before sharing.

**Share with your audience:**

1. Click **Share** in the top-right corner.
2. Click **Copy prototype link** at the bottom of the share panel.
3. Paste this link into your presentation email, conference submission, or lab website.

Your audience can open this link in any browser on any device — no software required. They can navigate freely, zoom into figures, and explore your research at their own pace.

> **For presenting live:** Click the Play button to launch fullscreen, then use your keyboard arrow keys or click to advance. The nav bar lets you jump to any section if the audience asks a question that's covered on a different screen.

---

## Self-check (2 min)

* Do you have **5 screens** with consistent nav bars on all of them?
* Does clicking the **Overview cards** jump to the correct screens?
* Does the **figure zoom** work — click to enlarge, click X to close?
* Did you test the prototype in **preview mode** before sharing?
* Is your **prototype link** shareable without requiring a Figma account to view?

